\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Finite Gaussian backgrounds and relabelings}
\date{2026-10-17}

\p{To compare two discrete Gaussian models, specify which data a change
of labels must carry. A \newvocab{finite Gaussian background} consists of a
\vocabk{finite incidence presentation}{fdip-0002}
#{(V,E,s,h)}, positive edge weights #{w:E\to\mathbb R_{>0}}, a mass
#{m>0}, and a nonempty proper retained subset #{R\subsetneq V}.
Put #{U=V\setminus R}; both index sets are nonempty.
The reference structures are the
\vocabk{counting inner products}{fdip-0002} on vertex and edge fields,
the \vocabk{weighted edge inner product}{fdip-0007} determined by #{w},
and [coordinate Lebesgue measure](fdip-002H) #{\lambda_V} on #{\mathbb R^V}.
Grouping the fields as #{(x,y)\in\mathbb R^R\times\mathbb R^U}
identifies #{\lambda_V} with the product #{dx\,dy}.}

\p{The incidence matrix #{B} and weight matrix #{W} determine the
\vocabk{weighted graph Laplacian}{fdip-0007} and
\vocabk{massive precision}{fdip-000A}:
##{L=B^TWB,\qquad Q=L+m^2I>0.}
The \vocabk{Gaussian probability law}{fdip-0005} #{\mu_Q} and its
\vocabk{retained marginal law}{fdip-000E} #{\mu_S} are then derived
data, with \vocabk{effective precision}{fdip-000D}
#{S=A-HC^{-1}H^T} in the retained/eliminated block ordering.
The Gaussian density in \citet{Proposition B.55}{friedli2017statistical},
p. 505, uses covariance #{\Sigma}; here #{\Sigma=Q^{-1}} by
\ref{fdip-000B}. A bare matrix omits the incidence data, reference
structures and retained subset of this background.}

\p{An \newvocab{exact relabeling} to a primed background is a pair of
bijections #{p:V\to V'} and #{\eta:E\to E'} such that
##{\begin{aligned}
s'\circ\eta&=p\circ s,&h'\circ\eta&=p\circ h,\\
w'\circ\eta&=w,&m'&=m,\\
p(R)&=R'.
\end{aligned}}
Thus directed endpoints, weights, mass and the partition are preserved.
In particular a loop's attachment vertex is preserved even though its
incidence row is zero. Define the coordinate maps #{P,J} by
##{\begin{aligned}
(P\phi)(p(v))&=\phi(v),\\
(Ja)(\eta(e))&=a(e).
\end{aligned}}
They preserve the counting pairings and satisfy
#{P^{-1}=P^T}, #{J^{-1}=J^T}. Weight preservation also gives
#{W'=JWJ^T}, so #{J} preserves the weighted edge pairing.}

\p{The endpoint equations imply the operator identities. At an edge
#{\eta(e)},
##{\begin{aligned}
(B'P\phi)_{\eta(e)}
 &=\phi(h(e))-\phi(s(e))\\
 &=(JB\phi)_{\eta(e)}.
\end{aligned}}
Hence #{B'P=JB}, or #{B'=JBP^T}. Substituting into the Laplacian gives
##{\begin{aligned}
L'&=(B')^TW'B'\\
  &=PB^TJ^TJWJ^TJBP^T\\
  &=PLP^T,\\
Q'&=PQP^T.
\end{aligned}}
The same maps carry the normalized states and their marginals by
\ref{fdip-000H}. Identity bijections satisfy all the conditions;
inverse bijections do too, and componentwise composition preserves each
endpoint, weight, mass and subset equation. Existence of an exact
relabeling therefore defines an equivalence relation on these backgrounds.}

\p{For a broader comparison, forget the mass, state and partition and
restrict to nonzero Laplacians with their counting pairings. Define
##{L\approx L'\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
L'=cPLP^T}
for some #{c>0} and vertex-coordinate permutation #{P}.
Identity uses #{(c,P)=(1,I)}; inverse uses #{(c^{-1},P^T)};
composition uses the product of the positive scalars and of the
permutations. This is another equivalence relation. Exact background
relabelings give its case #{c=1} when #{L\ne0}, but this broader
relation need not identify the fixed-mass Gaussian states.
The [trace ratio](fdip-000I) will separate two backgrounds even after
these extra identifications.}

\p{An arbitrary invertible real field map #{y=T\phi} is outside the
declared arrows. In these coordinates the precision becomes
#{T^{-T}QT^{-1}}, where #{T^{-T}=(T^{-1})^T}, and the original pairing
becomes #{y^TT^{-T}T^{-1}z}. The [reference Jacobian](fdip-002K) gives
#{dy=|\det T|\,d\phi}.
\citet{Proposition B.55}{friedli2017statistical} uses this substitution
in its proof.
Enlarging the comparison to such maps must carry these reference
structures explicitly.}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{Matrix.reindexLinearEquiv} and
\leanref{Matrix.reindexRingEquiv} express the linear and multiplicative
parts of coordinate reindexing. They support the matrix calculation;
the endpoint, weight, mass and partition requirements are additional
data of the exact relabeling above.}
